{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: Duplicate provider call, uncached `reviewThreads`, and guard ordering**

Three small things in this guard, none behavioral:

1. The two branches duplicate the identical `provider.reviewThreads(item.prNumber)` resolution — collapse to one call:
   ```js
   if (q.threadsResolved === true || q.threadsResolved === false) {
       var th = (provider && provider.reviewThreads) ? provider.reviewThreads(item.prNumber) : null;
       var ok = (q.threadsResolved === true)
           ? !!(th && th.total > 0 && th.unresolved === 0)
           : !!(th && th.unresolved > 0);
       if (!ok) return false;
   }
   ```
2. `provider.reviewThreads` (smProvider.js) is **not** routed through the per-tick ioCache, unlike `prStatus`/`listOpenPrs`. Every rule carrying `threadsResolved` now costs one live GraphQL call per candidate PR per tick — with this rule, every `ai_pr_reviewed` PR is a candidate. Worth memoizing like `prStatus`.
3. This check runs before the cheap `prMachineAuthor` gate, so a guest PR latched `ai_pr_reviewed` (the `review-external-once` path adds that label to guest PRs too) pays the threads call every tick before failing the machine-author check. Reordering the `prMachineAuthor` block above the thread guards is semantics-preserving (all guards are pure predicates) and fails that class fast.
